<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andong Kim clan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as New Andong Kim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andong">Andong</a> Kim clan was a Korean aristocratic lineage that dominated the government of Joseon for most of the first half of the nineteenth century. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeongjo-of-joseon">King Jeongjo</a> in 1800, its leader Kim Jo-sun married his daughter to the boy king Sunjo, and the clan used its queens to control appointments and revenue under three kings. Its in-law rule, marked by corruption and unrest, ended when the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heungseon-daewongun">Heungseon Daewongun</a> took power in 1864.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andong-kim-clan.jpg" alt="Andong Kim clan" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Team GOGI · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%EC%95%88%EB%8F%99_%EA%B9%80%EC%94%A8%EC%9D%98_%EC%A2%85%EB%AC%B8.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Andong Kim covers two lineages, the Old and the New Andong Kim clans, both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangban">yangban</a> families of Joseon that took their name from Andong in North Gyeongsang province and traced their descent to the Gyeongju Kim clan. The New Andong Kim clan regards as its founder Kim Sŏnp'yŏng, a castellan of Andong who was one of the merit subjects of the founding of the Goryeo dynasty and received a new surname from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goryeo">Taejo of Goryeo</a> for his part in it. It was his descendant Kim Sŭpdon who made him the clan's founder.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under Joseon the clan rose through official service. Kim Samgŭn (1390–1465) settled in the village of Sosan-ri in the 15th century, and it remained a clan village of the Andong Kim from then on. His grandson Kim Yŏngsu (1446–1502), whose pen name was Yangseodang, showed skill in martial arts early and was given an official post without taking the state examination, in recognition of his ancestors' merits. Kim Yŏngsu's great-great-grandson Kim Sanghŏn (1570–1652) served as minister of rites.</p><p>Members of the two clans passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwageo">gwageo</a> examinations in large numbers, and between them they gave Joseon three queen consorts: Queen Sunwon, Queen Hyohyeon and Queen Cheorin. The New Andong Kim became one of the most influential families of the late Joseon period. Its members included Kim Jo-sun, Kim Chwagŭn, Kim Chwa-chin and Kim Okkyun as well as two of those queens, Hyohyeon and Cheorin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The head house of the clan, the family home of Kim Yŏngsu at Sosan-ri, has been passed down among the male heirs of the line. It faces south-west and has no main gate, and all its buildings have tiled roofs. A men's quarters, gate quarters and women's quarters stand in a square around a central courtyard, with a shrine behind the women's quarters. The women's quarters has a wooden-floored hall between two rooms with underfloor heating, and the men's quarters can be opened into a single space by lifting the doors between its room and hall. In 2020 the New Andong Kim clan numbered 30,300 people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andong Kim clan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yeongjo (r. 1724–1776) and Jeongjo (r. 1776–1800) tried to balance the factions through a 'policy of impartiality' (tangpyeong), but in the nineteenth century royal in-law families, above all the <b>Andong Kim</b>, dominated government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
